  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 154 PM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nUpper ridge over the central CONUS to break down Saturday, as a\nstrong upper low arrives onshore in the Pacific Northwest. A few\nlead shortwaves will round the top of the flattening ridge\nproviding us with increasing temperatures, humidity levels, and\nprecipitation chances late weekend and into next week. The \nfirst shortwave to arrive Saturday, with recent model trends \nshowing a decaying line of showers/storms during the late \nmorning hours across northern and northeast IA. Forecast \nsoundings and time height cross sections show plenty of dry air \nfor this activity to overcome leading to light QPF amounts under\n0.10\" mainly confined to areas northwest of Cedar Rapids. After\nthis wave, recent model trends show the main storm track\nremaining to our northwest over the upper Midwest which would \nkeep us warm and dry for the rest of August. For now, will stick\nwith NBM slight chance/chance PoPs Sunday and Monday, but am \ninclined these will be removed in later forecasts. \n\nSunday onward, there is higher confidence of building heat and \nhumidity with 594 dam upper high centered over southern \nMissouri. 850 mb temps are progged to rise into the 21-25C range\nand combined with dewpints in the upper 60s/low 70s will result\nin highs in the upper 80s/low 90s and heat indices approaching \n100 degrees at times through Thursday. NBM probabilities of 70+ \ndegree dewpoints are in the 50-80% range Sunday through \nThursday. In addition, the latest CPC 6-10 day temperature \noutlook has 90% or higher combined probabilities of near/above \nnormal temperatures for the Sept 1st-5th timeframe. It looks\nlike late summer heat will arrive prior to the Labor Day \nweekend. However, as noted by the previous shift there remains a\nlarge temperature spread in high temps (6-9 degrees) next week \ndue to the strength and placement of upper ridge and any precip \nchances. For this reason, capped highs to remain under 93 \ndegrees and left the daily PoPs from the NBM alone. Depending on\nwhen the upper ridge slides east or retrogrades westward will \nbe key when we see precip chances and any relief from the heat\nas well as any severe risk. With large model variability, it is\nhard to pin this down at this time range.",
